   "name": "heldout-08",
   "wall_s": 20.689263343811035,
   "text": "# Why Compressed Data Trades CPU for Storage Traffic\n\n## The core mechanism\n\nCompression works by replacing redundant patterns with shorter representations. That transformation is not free:\n\n- **Decompression cost:** Every read must run the inverse transform (e.g., Huffman decoding, LZ77 back-references, entropy decoding) before the data is usable. This is pure added CPU work on the read path.\n- **Compression cost:** Every write must analyze the data and encode it, which is often *more* expensive than decompression (searching for matches, building dictionaries, entropy coding).\n- **Reduced bytes moved:** The payoff is that fewer bytes cross the bottleneck \u2014 disk I/O, network links, PCIe, or memory bandwidth. If that bottleneck is the limiting resource, total time-to-completion can drop even though CPU time rises.\n\nThe key insight is that **CPU and I/O are different resources**. If the workload is I/O-bound (or bandwidth-bound), spending idle CPU cycles to shrink the byte stream is nearly free and can be a large net win. If the workload is CPU-bound, the same trade is a loss.\n\nA useful mental model: total time \u2248 max(bytes / bandwidth, cycles / CPU_rate) plus serialization. Compression",
